WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.480
<v DANA>Someone who empties the bins on a production line told us this: "It wasn't my

2
00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.200
<v DANA>needle. I never even saw it go in the bin."

3
00:00:07.827 --> 00:00:08.800
<v DANA>I'm Dana.

4
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:09.840
<v RAY>And I'm Ray.

5
00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:18.490
<v DANA>Ray, you've worked at a few different plants over the years.

6
00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:21.570
<v DANA>What changes for you, walking into a new one?

7
00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.490
<v RAY>Everything you can see changes...

8
00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:25.810
<v RAY>the machines, the layout, the people.

9
00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:30.850
<v RAY>But I still catch myself looking for the same small habits right away, because they

10
00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.850
<v RAY>tell me more than any sign on the wall.

11
00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:37.810
<v DANA>Small habits are where today's subject lives, too: the needles and other sharps

12
00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:40.130
<v DANA>people use and throw away at work.

13
00:00:41.933 --> 00:00:44.920
<v DANA>A guy on the line takes insulin on his break.

14
00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:48.360
<v DANA>He's at a table in the break room with the syringe in his hand.

15
00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:52.840
<v DANA>Needles always felt like a hospital thing to me, so why is that syringe your

16
00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:54.440
<v DANA>business on a plant floor?

17
00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:59.080
<v RAY>Because it's a sharp, and a sharp is any medical needle, or anything else that can

18
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:03.800
<v RAY>cut or puncture skin. Needlesticks are especially common in healthcare.

19
00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.200
<v RAY>But on plant floors they're a real and growing risk, as more people give themselves

20
00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:11.560
<v RAY>shots for diabetes, allergies and other conditions.

21
00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:15.720
<v DANA>And once he's used it, why does that one needle still worry you?

22
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:17.720
<v RAY>It still holds a little blood.

23
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.720
<v RAY>And it's so sharp that chances are high it sticks someone else by accident and

24
00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:23.560
<v RAY>exposes them to that blood.

25
00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:26.920
<v DANA>That's a lot of risk in something so small.

26
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.720
<v DANA>Let's turn this to you, listening on your shift...

27
00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:33.080
<v DANA>before he takes the cap off, what should he do first?

28
00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:38.930
<v DANA>Okay, Ray, tell us what he should do first.

29
00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:43.530
<v RAY>He plans FIRST, how he'll handle the needle and where it goes after.

30
00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:47.770
<v RAY>For him, that means finding the slot on the sharps container on the break-room wall.

31
00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:52.170
<v DANA>So he's done, and his hands are full with the syringe and his lunch.

32
00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:54.570
<v DANA>There's a trash bin right under the table.

33
00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:55.610
<v DANA>What do you tell him?

34
00:01:55.770 --> 00:02:00.730
<v RAY>Not that bin. If there's a needle-free option, he uses that in the first place.

35
00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:03.770
<v RAY>If the syringe has a safety feature, he turns it on.

36
00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:06.570
<v RAY>Then it goes in that container immediately.

37
00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:11.210
<v DANA>Immediately. If he worked in a clinic instead, what would you tell him then?

38
00:02:11.578 --> 00:02:16.810
<v RAY>In healthcare settings, used needles can't be bent, broken, recapped, removed or

39
00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:21.530
<v RAY>separated from the syringe. They go in a sharps container immediately or as soon as

40
00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:22.330
<v RAY>possible.

41
00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:24.650
<v DANA>So what's your short version for him?

42
00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:28.650
<v RAY>He knows where it's going before he starts, the same as he would before cleaning up

43
00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:33.530
<v RAY>broken glass. He uses a needle-free option if there is one, turns the safety feature

44
00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:36.170
<v RAY>on, and drops it in that container right away.

45
00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:39.530
<v DANA>Next coffee run, I'm looking for that box on the wall.

46
00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:46.080
<v RAY>That container on the wall only works if the needle actually ends up inside it.

47
00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.320
<v DANA>So let's stay with one that didn't.

48
00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:54.790
<v DANA>Every bag of trash on a production line ends up in somebody's hands.

49
00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:59.430
<v DANA>Jacob empties the bins along the line when each shift ends, and he's here to tell

50
00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:01.710
<v DANA>you about one night on that job.

51
00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:05.270
<v JACOB>The last bin I empty is by the loading door.

52
00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:11.350
<v JACOB>Same every night. That night, I lifted the liner out and went to tie it off.

53
00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:16.790
<v JACOB>Then I saw it. A needle tip, poking through the plastic, right where my hand was

54
00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:20.870
<v JACOB>going. I let go. I just stood there.

55
00:03:21.163 --> 00:03:25.430
<v JACOB>It wasn't my needle. I never even saw it go in the bin.

56
00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:28.150
<v JACOB>Walking out, I passed the break room.

57
00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:31.430
<v JACOB>The sharps container was up on the wall, same as always.

58
00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:36.001
<v DANA>That close. Tell me what he'd have been facing if it had stuck him.

59
00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:40.950
<v RAY>A stick can expose you to bloodborne pathogens, meaning germs carried in blood, like

60
00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:44.670
<v RAY>H-I-V, hepatitis B and hepatitis C.

61
00:03:44.710 --> 00:03:47.550
<v RAY>Those could lead to severe health complications.

62
00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:52.110
<v DANA>Some people figure a used needle is only a risk to the person who used it.

63
00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:53.510
<v DANA>How do you answer that?

64
00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:58.030
<v RAY>A needle that isn't thrown away properly can hurt someone further down the line,

65
00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:01.670
<v RAY>like the person handling the trash, who NEVER touched it.

66
00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:05.750
<v RAY>So the way you get rid of a sharp directly affects someone else's risk of getting

67
00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:11.630
<v RAY>hurt. And it only takes one shortcut, on one busy day, to cause a

68
00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:16.470
<v RAY>serious injury... maybe an infection that changes a life.

69
00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:22.070
<v DANA>I keep seeing that bag by the loading door, and his hand already reaching to tie it.

70
00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:27.020
<v RAY>A trash bin by the door and a container on the wall.

71
00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:29.340
<v RAY>Only one of them was made to hold a needle.

72
00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:34.610
<v DANA>A needle can push through the fingertip of a thick work glove.

73
00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:41.090
<v DANA>So say a plant has to choose. Thicker gloves for everyone who handles the trash, or

74
00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:44.770
<v DANA>more sharps containers, like the kind mounted on a break-room wall.

75
00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:47.930
<v DANA>I'd have bought the gloves. Which would you pick?

76
00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:53.890
<v RAY>The containers. Gloves are P-P-E, the protective gear you wear, and P-P-E alone

77
00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:57.650
<v RAY>works less well against needlesticks than engineering controls.

78
00:04:57.730 --> 00:05:01.410
<v RAY>An engineering control is something built into the workplace, like that container on

79
00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:05.890
<v RAY>the wall. The best protection is a proper sharps container, because it takes the

80
00:05:05.997 --> 00:05:08.242
<v RAY>hazard away before anyone can touch it.

81
00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:11.090
<v DANA>Then those gloves would just be for show?

82
00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:16.450
<v RAY>No, they earn their place. Heavy-duty gloves made of nitrile, latex, vinyl or

83
00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:20.850
<v RAY>neoprene can protect your hands from cuts and from hazardous substances.

84
00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:23.170
<v RAY>I just wouldn't count on them against a needle.

85
00:05:23.170 --> 00:05:27.010
<v DANA>Walk me through what the container actually changes out on a plant floor.

86
00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:32.410
<v RAY>A safety-engineered sharps container closes the sharp inside the moment it drops in.

87
00:05:32.450 --> 00:05:36.530
<v RAY>In manufacturing settings, that cuts down on accidental needlesticks, and on

88
00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:39.650
<v RAY>bloodborne pathogens passing from one person to another.

89
00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:43.650
<v DANA>So once the containers are up, do they do the job by themselves?

90
00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:49.090
<v RAY>No. People lean toward the easiest path, and the containers and the training both

91
00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:54.130
<v RAY>have to beat that habit. At more than one plant, I've watched people walk RIGHT past

92
00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:56.130
<v RAY>a container to the nearest trash bin.

93
00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:01.160
<v DANA>Think about the gloves you pull on at the start of a shift.

94
00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:04.560
<v RAY>Now think about the sharps container closest to where you work.

95
00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:07.320
<v RAY>Which one would you trust with a used needle?
